ant nexus for metastasis, evaluation, and interpretation of lab chemistry\",\"explanation\":\"Bones are described as clinically important sites in cancer spread and in blood/marrow health, and are noted as potential metastatic destinations for several cancers, including prostate cancer (including advanced disease) and bladder cancer (including stage 4B metastatic disease). This makes bones a cross-cutting entity linking multiple cancer types and stages within the community. [Data: Entities (133)]\\n\\nThe bones entity also highlights clinical evaluation contexts, including imaging approaches such as bone scans and MRI, and notes that bone scans can show increased tracer uptake (“bright spots”) that may reflect bone damage due to metastases, treatment, or other non-cancer problems. This underscores the interpretive complexity of bone-related findings and why bones frequently appear in assessment pathways. [Data: Entities (133)]\\n\\nA specific relationship connects bones to laboratory interpretation: the blood chemistry profile measures chemicals affected by bones and other tissues. This positions bones as an influence on common clinical labs, complementing their role as metastatic sites. [Data: Relationships (3595)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Dietary intake of fruits and vegetables is associated with lower risk of developing advanced-stage prostate cancer\",\"explanation\":\"Fruits and vegetables are explicitly described as foods associated with a lower risk of developing advanced prostate cancer when consumed more often. This introduces a modifiable factor into a community otherwise dominated by disease stage and metastasis concepts. [Data: Entities (3397)]\\n\\nA corresponding relationship states that eating more fruits and vegetables is associated with a lower risk of developing advanced-stage prostate cancer. This creates a direct connection between a lifestyle factor (diet) and the advanced-stage disease entity. [Data: Relationships (6774)]\\n\\nThe dataset does not pro